.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL-ECPARAM 1ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl-ecparam
.Nd EC parameter manipulation and generation
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm openssl ecparam
.Oo
.Fl help ]
.Oo
.Fl inform
.Ar DER Ns | Ns Ar PEM ]
.Oo
.Fl outform
.Ar DER Ns | Ns Ar PEM ]
.Oo
.Fl in
.Em filename ]
.Oo
.Fl out
.Em filename ]
.Oo
.Fl noout ]
.Oo
.Fl text ]
.Oo
.Fl check ]
.Oo
.Fl check_named ]
.Oo
.Fl name
.Em arg ]
.Oo
.Fl list_curves ]
.Oo
.Fl conv_form
.Em arg ]
.Oo
.Fl param_enc
.Em arg ]
.Oo
.Fl no_seed ]
.Oo
.Fl genkey ]
.Oo
.Fl engine
.Em id ]
.Oo
.Fl rand
.Em files ]
.Oo
.Fl writerand
.Em file ]
.Oo
.Fl provider
.Em name ]
.Oo
.Fl provider-path
.Em path ]
.Oo
.Fl provparam
.Em [name:]key=value ]
.Oo
.Fl propquery
.Em propq ]
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command is used to manipulate or generate EC parameter files.
.Pp
OpenSSL is currently not able to generate new groups and therefore this
command can only create EC parameters from known (named) curves.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -inform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The EC parameters input format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -outform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The EC parameters output format; the default is
.Sy PEM .
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.Pp
Parameters are encoded as
.Sy EcpkParameters
as specified in IETF RFC 3279.
.It Sy -in Em filename
This specifies the input file to read parameters from or standard input
if this option is not specified.
.It Sy -out Em filename
This specifies the output filename parameters to.
Standard output is used if this option is not present.
The output filename can be the same as the input filename, which leads
to replacing the file contents.
Note that file I/O is not atomic.
The output file is truncated and then written.
.It Sy -noout
This option inhibits the output of the encoded version of the
parameters.
.It Sy -text
This option prints out the EC parameters in human readable form.
.It Sy -check
Validate the elliptic curve parameters.
.It Sy -check_named
Validate the elliptic name curve parameters by checking if the curve
parameters match any built-in curves.
.It Sy -name Em arg
Use the EC parameters with the specified 'short' name.
Use
.Sy -list_curves
to get a list of all currently implemented EC parameters.
.It Sy -list_curves
Print out a list of all currently implemented EC parameters names and
exit.
.It Sy -conv_form Em arg
This specifies how the points on the elliptic curve are converted into
octet strings.
Possible values are:
.Sy compressed ,
.Sy uncompressed
(the default value) and
.Sy hybrid .
For more information regarding the point conversion forms please read
the X9.62 standard.
.Sy Note
Due to patent issues the
.Sy compressed
option is disabled by default for binary curves and can be enabled by
defining the preprocessor macro
.Sy OPENSSL_EC_BIN_PT_COMP
at compile time.
.It Sy -param_enc Em arg
This specifies how the elliptic curve parameters are encoded.
Possible value are:
.Sy named_curve ,
i.e.
the ec parameters are specified by an OID, or
.Sy explicit
where the ec parameters are explicitly given (see RFC 3279 for the
definition of the EC parameters structures).
The default value is
.Sy named_curve .
.Sy Note
the
.Sy implicitlyCA
alternative, as specified in RFC 3279, is currently not implemented in
OpenSSL.
.It Sy -no_seed
This option inhibits that the 'seed' for the parameter generation is
included in the ECParameters structure (see RFC 3279).
.It Sy -genkey
This option will generate an EC private key using the specified
parameters.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Pp
The
.Xr openssl-genpkey 1
and
.Xr openssl-pkeyparam 1
commands are capable of performing all the operations this command can,
as well as supporting other public key types.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The documentation for the
.Xr openssl-genpkey 1
and
.Xr openssl-pkeyparam 1
commands contains examples equivalent to the ones listed here.
.Pp
To create EC parameters with the group 'prime192v1':
.Bd -literal
  openssl ecparam -out ec_param.pem -name prime192v1
.Ed
.Pp
To create EC parameters with explicit parameters:
.Bd -literal
  openssl ecparam -out ec_param.pem -name prime192v1 -param_enc explicit
.Ed
.Pp
To validate given EC parameters:
.Bd -literal
  openssl ecparam -in ec_param.pem -check
.Ed
.Pp
To create EC parameters and a private key:
.Bd -literal
  openssl ecparam -out ec_key.pem -name prime192v1 -genkey
.Ed
.Pp
To change the point encoding to 'compressed':
.Bd -literal
  openssl ecparam -in ec_in.pem -out ec_out.pem -conv_form compressed
.Ed
.Pp
To print out the EC parameters to standard output:
.Bd -literal
  openssl ecparam -in ec_param.pem -noout -text
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr openssl-pkeyparam 1 ,
.Xr openssl-genpkey 1 ,
.Xr openssl-ec 1 ,
.Xr openssl-dsaparam 1
